n to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: administrative level\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description: <br>1. King<br><br>_ Central government line _<br>2. Highest ranking financial official<br>3. dioikekes (financial official)  (but not highest ranking)Central elite split over Ptolemais and Alexandria or Memphis<br>dioikekes had "an army of subordinates" (not listed) who presumably top 5 administrative levels? 4. eklogistes (accountant) or later idios logos (privy purse) <br>5. ...\xa0? ...6. ...\xa0? ...<br>_ Provincial line _<br>2. Governor of the Northbased at central government<br>2. Governor of the Southbased at central government. Ptolemais.<br>3. Strategoi district governorsin total 40 administration districts called oikononos <br>4. Royal land / Remitted land / land held by cleruchs / land held in gift / private land / city land Lloyd (2000) lists these all at the same level.<br>4. Mayor of towns and mayors of villages. Komogrammtan epistates.<br>5. Sometimes (depending on region) the mayors of towns and of villages are part of the same administrative level; sometimes the mayors of villages report to mayors of towns and thus constitute two separate administrative levels.<br>5. Village elders and local priesthoods - epistoles in temples <ul><li><ul><li><ul><li>This overview is not necessarily reflecting reality for the whole of Egypt and for the whole of the period. Also, the data is tied to specific locations and too patchy for a general rule. However, as a rule the data represents the period after 280/270 better than the period 305bc-270bc. ***</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><br>_Alexandria_<br>Population Greeks and non-Greeks (native Egyptians, foreign immigrants)<br>\xa0?. City governor"A royal official "in charge of the city" ... is attested in the third century B.C. and later. This was a civilian rather than a military appointment." <br>\xa0?. Secretary of the Council"A fragmentary inscription dated to the mid-third century B.C. records a decree that provides evidence for the existence of a boule, secretary of the council, ekklesia" <br>\xa0?. Tribes"The Alexandrian citizens were organized into tribes, demes, and phratries. It would appear - based on a papyrus of c. 265 B.C., which probably refers to Alexandria - that there were 5 tribes, 60 demes, and 720 phratries. It has been suggested that the five tribes corresponded to the five quarters in the city. Three tribal names are known: the dynastic names Berenike and Ptolemais. as well as Dionysia." <br>\xa0?. Demes<br>\xa0?. Phratries<br>_Ptolemais in the Thebaid_<br>Described by Strabo as "the largest city of the Thebaid; he added that it was no smaller than Memphis and that its constitution was in the Greek manner... The papyrological and epigraphic evidence bears out Strabo\'s observation about the government." <br>Boule (Council of Citizens)Evidence for "decrees passed by the boule and demos" as well as ekklesia (assembly), prytaneis (executives of the boule), prytaneion, archiprytanis<br>grammateus (scribe?)<br>agones, agonothetes (president of the sacred games)<br>gymnasiarch  (supervised games and contests)<br>tribes and demes.<br>\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1851, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1781})
