 could not afford to maintain a large standing army or an elaborate administrative organization."<br>The rough hierarchy may have been as follows:<br>1. Emperor <br>_Court_<br>2. Royal officialsIncluding, among others, the king\'s attendants and advisors, the <i>mahasenapati</i> or army commander, the superintendent of stores, the treasurer, officials tasked with drafting and registering the king\'s documents, and officials tasked with supervising feudal lords <br>3.4.<br>_Provincial Government_<br>2. <i>Mahabhojas</i> and <i>maharathis</i>Feudal lords who were blood relatives of the royal family .<br>2. <i>Rajas</i> Other feudal lords (specifically rajas, who struck coins in their own name, and mahasenapatis, military governors posted at outlying centres)Feudal lords who struck coins in their own name .<br>2. <i>Mahasenapatis</i>Army commanders were sometimes put in charge of governing outlying centres .<br>3. Local administrators<i>Bhojakas</i>, <i>uparikas</i>, <i>gaulmikas</i>, <i>patipalakas</i> .<br>4. <i>Gramanis</i>Village officials in charge of five, sometimes ten villages .<br>5. Village assemblies .<br>NOTE: Sources are often unclear and describe the hierarchy in slightly different ways.<br>\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1466, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1398})
