ith Administrative levels. Start with the commander-in-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "\n                        Variable name: administrative level\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description:  levels.<br>1. Sultan<br>The Saadis had an Ottoman-style palace government ruled by a Sultan.<br>_Central government_<br>2. WazirAlso known as the viceroy, governor of Fez, crown prince, or vizir.<br>2. Sultan's councilComprising the chancellor of the seal, the chancellor in charge of protocol and ceremony, one in charge of the Sultan's horses and camels, and one in charge of administration and division of rents and taxes<br>3. bureaucrat in charge of rents <i>inferred level</i>4. Scribe or sub-manager<br>3. bureaucrat in charge of taxes <i>inferred level</i>4. Scribe or sub-manager <i>inferred level</i>5. Tax collector<br>2. First secretaryHead of Sultan's council, secretary of state, majordomo, treasurer<br>2. Qadi al-qudatThe main qadi, head of the judiciary, whose task it was to assign qadis to different cities and regions.<br>Petty bureaucrats<br>Tax collectors, clerics, secretaries, and qadis.<br>_Regional government_<br>2.<br>3.<br>4.<br>\n                    "}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1349, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1257})
