tary levels of a polity. Same principle as with Administrative levels. Start with the commander-in-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: administrative level\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description:  levels.<br>1. King of kings (mansa)<br>king had title Mansa <br>"As in other African empires, the supreme ruler was a king of kings." <br>The ruler "bore the military title of mansa, conqueror." <br>"The mansas adopted the Ghanaian and Sudanic concepts of kingship to institutionalize their power." <br>"The rulers surrounded themselves with a bodyguard, servants, and elaborate ceremonies."<br>_Central government_<br>2. Head of the imperial council or one of the officials of the imperial councilthere was an "imperial council" <br>late 14th century government characterised by rule of powerful government officials and a sidelined monarch <br>3. Government officialsAfter his pilgrimage to Cairo and Mecca "Mansa Musa returned to Mali with Arab and Berber adventurers to serve in his administration."<br>Santigui (master of the treasury) <br>4. Scribes"court circle included clerics and lawyers literate in Arabic" <br>4. State farms official"Client clans, castes of dependent craftsmen, and people allied by marriage or by past service supported the ruler. Slaves and serfs worked in agricultural settlements to provide produce for the court, the army, and the administration." <br>royal slaves worked in "settled colonies" in the inland delta region. "each had to produce a quota of grain for collection by boat at the appointed season."<br>_Mande chiefdoms_<br>2. Chief (fama) of a kafu (or kafts)"Mande-speaking ethnic core" <br>"Mande-speaking peoples lived in family and village units, the head of the family being both priest and chieftain. A group of villages in turn formed a kafts, or kafu, a community of 1,000 to 15,000 people living around a mud-walled town and ruled by a hereditary chieftain called a fama."<br>"Within the Mande-speaking heartland the basic building-block of government was the kafu, a community of anything from 1000 to 15,000 people living in or near a mud-walled town and ruled by a hereditary dynast called a fama."<br>Mema was a province in the Mali Empire <br>the paramount ruler "bore the military title of mansa, "conquerer", which underlined the reality that his dominion might expand or contract according to the range of his armed forces. Where the mansa\'s soldiers were no longer seen, there the kafus would soon resume their independence under their traditional famas." <br>Oral tradition "Sunjata Epic" says Mali Empire founded by Sunjata Keita. Initially there was a Mande Chiefdom in Farakoro. The chief had the title maghan. Sunjata "organized the soldiers of all the Mande chiefdoms into a powerful army. They went to war against Susu." The unified Mande chiefdoms formed the basis of the Mali Empire. <br>3. Village headman"Mande-speaking peoples lived in family and village units, the head of the family being both priest and chieftain. A group of villages in turn formed a kafts, or kafu, a community of 1,000 to 15,000 people living around a mud-walled town and ruled by a hereditary chieftain alled a fama."<br>_Vassal kingdoms_<br>2. Vassal king or chief"Outside the Mande-speaking nucleus, the relationship with subordinate rulers was even more essentially based upon the regular or occasional payment of tribute."<br>Mid-13th century: "the Wolof and the Fulbe recognised its paramountcy" and gave tribute <br>ruler of Mali received tribute from lesser kings and chiefs. <br>Mansa Musa reigned 1312-1337 CE. Ibn Kathir (c1300-c1374 CE) sad he ruled over 24 other kings.  Al-Umari said Musa had "conquered 24 cities, each with its surrounding district with villages and estates" and that he had a palace <br>"In Mali, as in other African empires, the supreme ruler was essentially a paramount, a king of kings, the degree of whose authority varied greatly from one part of his dominions to another, according to the accessibility of each to the imperial armies and tax collectors." <br>3. District<br>4. Villages<br>\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2019, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1926})
